@davidfowl

It is more than that. 
For example, Unity can resolve structs (if they are not ref). It is the only container that does it. So, if constructor has struct parameter Unity will succeed, but default will fail. Other containers might have different peculiarities.

From the original description of the use case, it was more about things like `ActivatorUtilities` determining whether it should even _try_ to resolve the thing; which indicates to me `IsRegistered` is the intent here, not `IsResolvable`.

> #46132 - This would allow the logic in ActivatorUtilities to pick the constructor based on what services are registered in the container.

With the dynamic nature of registration and resolution - from lambda expressions to registrations that can be added on the fly in child lifetime scopes - there's not a reliable way for Autofac to calculate whether something can be resolved without actually _doing the resolution_. If the goal is to determine if it's resolvable, that likely won't be something we can support. If the goal is to query whether the registration is present, that's something we can do.

For example, consider a registration like this:

```c#
var builder = new ContainerBuilder();
builder.Register(ctx => {
  var config = ctx.Resolve<IConfiguration>();
  var section = config.GetSection("data");
  if(section == null) {
    throw new InvalidOperationException("Missing configuration.");
  }
  return new Component(config["value"]);
}).As<IComponent>();
var container = builder.Build();
```

I can tell you that `IComponent` _is registered_. I can't really tell you that it's not actually _resolvable_ because there's logic involved doing service location in an imperative fashion. Even if it were, I can't tell you if the configuration that's required is there and would cause the resolution to fail.

(It may appear to be a contrived use case, but it's actually pretty common to see stuff like this.)

Point being - **there's a significant difference between `IsRegistered` and `IsResolvable`**.

I'd argue it's a _huge_ difference. I can't tell you if you _can actually get it_ and the method isn't `CanProbablyGetServiceButNotGuaranteed<T>()` so it seems like `CanGetService` or `IsResolvable` isn't promising on its name. `Can` and `Is` have connotations - they're Boolean. You _can_ or you _can't_; it's not quantum.

I mean, you could switch it be tri-state return value - "guaranteed you can get this," "you might be able to get this but you won't know until you try," "don't even try to get this" - but that seems more complex than what the original intent is. (Likely Autofac would end up returning the "you might be able to get this" based on `IsRegistered` and never guarantee anything.)

I hope this doesn't come down to bikeshedding on the name; I'm just really frustrated when I run across an API that supposedly has a sort of safety check ("Can I do this?") and it tells me everything's going to be good, then I do the thing it just said I could do and the thing fails.

By way of comparison, this would really suck:

```c#
var t = new Dictionary<string, string>();
lock(syncroot) {
  if(t.ContainsKey("key")) {
    // If this fails - even if I've locked around it and it's not
    // a case where someone yanked the key out from under
    // me, I'd be irritated.
    Console.WriteLine(t["key"]);
  }
}
```

> We want to know without incurring side effects what types a container will return null from GetService for instead of returning an instance or throwing due to misconfiguration.

Well, that sounds like `CanResolve` or `CanGetService` then, and the only way to do that, at least in Autofac, is to actually do the resolution. @alistairjevans can correct me on this one, but we can tell you easily if it's registered; and absolutely can't tell you if you're going to succeed at resolving without doing the operation.

I'm not sure how MEDI verifies this will work at all:

```c#
